## Is sleep apnoea a disability?

Under the **Equality Act 2010**, a condition counts as a disability if it has a **substantial** (more than minor) and **long-term** (12 months or more) effect on your normal daily activities. The law looks at how you would be **without treatment**. OSA that is well controlled with CPAP may not be covered, but OSA that still causes severe tiredness can be.

## At work

Tiredness can affect concentration and safety. Reasonable adjustments could include:

- flexible start times, or avoiding night shifts
- regular breaks
- avoiding safety critical tasks until your treatment is working
- time off for sleep clinic appointments

If you **drive for work**, you must follow the DVLA rules, and tell your employer if you cannot drive for a while.

## Benefits

OSA on its own does not usually qualify for PIP, as treatment usually works well. If you have other conditions, such as heart disease, COPD or obesity, that affect your daily life, the help you can get depends on those.

{% checker tool="pip" /%}

Free, independent advisers, such as **Citizens Advice** or a local welfare rights service, can help you check what you can claim and fill in forms.
